Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Coronal Holes Swallowing The Iron Parade
- Story & Context: Recorded live on the salt pans of Coquimbo during the two-minute eclipse of 1994, this track captures the precise moment the carnival's lead blacksmith, Ignis Vane, lost three fingers to a misfired steam-powered calliope. The sound of his blood splashing onto a heated iron cowbell was caught on the overhead room mics, causing the band to pivot from a traditional brass march into an agonizing 13/8 time signature crawl. The track captures the raw panic of the audience as the sun disappeared completely, leaving only screaming horns and low-end bass resonance to guide them through the darkness. Vane roared into the ambient mic, "The shadow doesn't ask for your eyes, it takes your blood," establishing the brutal, sun-choking manifesto of the genre.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Stark minimalist album art depicting a blackened solar corona casting sharp dark rays over an abandoned, rusting carnival carousel, painted in thick charcoal textures and blood-red highlights.

Track 2: Umbral-Struktur
- Story & Context: In a drafty, abandoned railway shed near Antofagasta, saxophone player Doctora Clara Haze locked herself in a disused train car for fourteen hours with only a tuned set of heavy cowbells and her brass instrument to confess her secret sabotage of the local observatory's optics. The track is an unhinged, two-part dialogue between a screeching, free-form saxophone solo and an asymmetrical, crushing progressive metal guitar riff that mimics the rhythm of a failing telescope gear. Halfway through the session, a sudden hailstorm battered the corrugated iron roof, an accidental acoustic element that the engineers chose to retain in the final mix. Haze’s raspy, whispered hook, "Zero degrees of light is the only truth the lens can hold," echoes hauntingly through the bone-dry rhythm section.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Surrealist architectural sketch of a decaying astronomical observatory with its dome torn open, revealing a inner mechanism made entirely of brass horns and rusted iron cowbells under a stark, starless sky.

Track 3: What Did The Blacksmith See In The Corona?
- Story & Context: Long after the eclipse ended and the desert sands cooled, local rumors persisted that the carnival troupe never actually packed up their gear, vanishing instead into the dry salt caverns beneath the valley. This track serves as the folklore finale of the EP, featuring a nine-minute, unstructured free-jazz breakdown where four different cowbell pitches fight for spatial dominance against a wall of distorted 8-string guitar feedback. The track ends abruptly when the sound of an iron door slamming shut cuts off the master tape, leaving only a low-frequency hum lingering in the dark. The final lyric, spoken by an unknown child performer over the silence, asks, "Did the light run away, or did we finally catch it?"
